  "solution": {
    "approach": "The Fibonacci sequence can be defined recursively: F(1)=0, F(2)=1, and for n>2, F(n)=F(n-1)+F(n-2). Direct recursion recomputes the same values many times, leading to exponential time. By storing already computed values (memoization) in an array of size 31 we ensure each value is computed once, giving linear time per test case. The memo array is initialized with -1 and the recursive function returns the stored result when available. This fits the problem constraints (N≤30) easily.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(N) per test case (N≤30), overall O(T·N).",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(N) for the memoization array (size 31).",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;import java.util.*;public class Main{static long[] dp=new long[31];static long fib(int n){if(n==1)return 0;if(n==2)return 1;if(dp[n]!=-1)return dp[n];dp[n]=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2);return dp[n];}public static void main(String[] args)throws Exception{BufferedReader br=new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));int T=Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());Arrays.fill(dp,-1);StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();for(int i=0;i<T;i++){int n=Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());sb.append(fib(n)).append('\\n');}System.out.print(sb.toString());}}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
